WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.152 --> 00:00:01.430
(anxious music)

00:00:01.430 --> 00:00:02.770
- [Reporter] Take a look at this map.

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:05.090
It shows the change in coronavirus cases

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:06.670
over the past two months.

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:08.790
You can see cases arising.

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:12.010
And as the weather gets colder
and people had back indoors,

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:14.140
experts say it could get worse.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.810
- As we get through the
fall and into the winter

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:18.760
with the holiday season going,

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.020
we've got to do something different.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.750
- [Reporter] Experts
warn that many buildings,

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:25.050
like restaurants, schools, and homes,

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:28.150
are not equipped with the
ventilation and filtration systems

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:31.020
needed to reduce the spread of COVID-19.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.530
- So, by now, people are really familiar

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:35.020
with masking, hand-washing, distancing,

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.610
but fewer people are
aware that ventilation

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:39.060
and filtration really matter.

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:42.730
- [Reporter] It's now
clear that the coronavirus

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:43.910
travels through the air,

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:46.530
which is why indoor
gatherings can be so deadly.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:48.820
The virus can accumulate in aerosols,

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.780
tiny droplets that gather in the air,

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:52.380
as we breathe and talk.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:55.860
- That virus is never naked
in the air, is what we say.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.960
It's always floating
in respiratory droplets

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.000
that are much bigger.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.240
- [Reporter] Indoors,
those particles can add up,

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.150
increasing the risk of transmission.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:05.700
And one way to mitigate that

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.220
is to pump in fresh air
with a ventilation system.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:10.610
- We have to think about
the thermal conditions

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:11.930
like humidity and temperature,

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:14.570
but also just how much
outdoor air is moving in.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:17.880
For me, the priority is to
increase the air exchange rate.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.090
- [Reporter] The air exchange rate

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:21.000
determines how many times an hour

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.070
fresh air circulates through a building.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.660
There's not an exact recommended rate,

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.580
but experts say that three
to six exchanges per hour

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:28.640
is ideal.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.930
But good flow of fresh
air is only one part

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:33.660
of preventing the indoor
spread of the coronavirus.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.360
The other is filters,

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.400
and experts recommend
using one called HEPA.

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.340
This type of pleated mechanical filter

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:43.100
is made from fiberglass, foam or cotton.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:46.320
It can remove more than
99% of airborne particles,

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.560
including those carrying
viruses like the coronavirus.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.080
This is the same type of filter

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.150
used in hospitals and airplanes,

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:54.590
but there has been some confusion

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:56.630
over whether or not HEPA filters

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:59.790
can effectively capture
airborne coronavirus.

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:02.290
That's because these filters
are rated for particles

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:06.640
that are 0.3 microns in size,
larger than the coronavirus.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.170
People assume that they
don't catch anything smaller,

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:10.730
but that's just not true.

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:12.790
- Filters are rated for the particle size

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:15.860
they perform worst at,
and that's 0.3 microns.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:19.730
As you get to bigger particles,
and even smaller particles,

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:23.520
the HEPA filter will
capture closer to 100%.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.560
- [Reporter] Because the
coronavirus tends to travel

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.080
in droplets that are
larger than 0.3 microns,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.760
HEPA filters can capture
almost all of them.

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.220
The virus also travels in
droplets that are smaller,

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:35.370
and HEPA filters can capture those too.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:38.290
- Filters work not by straining out things

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:40.290
that are larger than the holes in them,

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.820
but they actually work by
trapping particles in the air

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:47.300
as the air flows around the
different fibers in the filter.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:48.720
- [Reporter] When very small aerosols

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:51.990
bump into gas particles, they
move in a random pattern.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:53.760
That is called Brownian motion.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:57.520
- Kind of like a drunk person
stumbling around in the dark.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.650
And because of that random motion,

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.700
they can crash into the fibers.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:03.190
- [Reporter] The harder
it is for particles

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:05.450
carrying viruses to pass through a filter,

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:07.730
the lower the chance
of viral transmission.

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:09.590
But the problem is that not every building

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:11.410
has a system that can handle them.

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:14.290
- When you have a air system moving air

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:16.410
through the building, and
then it runs through a filter,

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:19.640
you can imagine that a
higher-efficiency filter

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:20.850
that's more tightly woven

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.640
is going to be harder to push air through.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:26.320
Very few systems can push
air through a HEPA filter

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:27.410
unless it's designed that way,

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:30.740
because HEPA is the most
efficient filter we have.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:31.990
- [Reporter] That is why experts recommend

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:34.230
that schools, businesses and homes

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:37.640
use a different type of
lower-efficiency filter called MERV.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:40.410
- If you can get up to
something called MERV-13,

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:43.290
that's able to remove 80% or more

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:45.980
of viruses that might be in the air.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:48.300
- [Reporter] This is important
because this type of filter

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.760
can be installed in standard
ventilation systems,

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:52.670
like what you might have in your home.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:54.390
Still, there are plenty of buildings

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:57.190
that aren't able to adapt
this type of filter either.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:03:59.270
- Many people are starting
to realize for the first time

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:01.110
that their buildings can't respond.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:03.560
Their systems are not dynamic,
they're not resilient,

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:05.730
they can't increase capacity.

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:07.470
- [Reporter] Older and underfunded schools

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:09.340
face some of the biggest challenges.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:12.150
According to the U.S. Government
Accountability Office,

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:15.420
41% of districts need to update or replace

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:18.410
their ventilation system in at
least half of their schools,

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:22.270
which adds up to about
36,000 schools nationwide.

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:24.750
The Washington D.C. public school system

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:27.250
spent $24 million on upgrades

00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:29.530
to handle higher-efficiency filters

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:32.730
in preparation for the return
of students and teachers.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.660
And it's not just pricey for schools.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:37.510
Some restaurants that will
need to spend over $30,000

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:41.060
to upgrade their systems, in
addition to operating costs.

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:43.360
- You pay more for energy

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:45.440
to bring in more outdoor
air and condition it,

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.640
and also work harder, your
system will work harder

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.550
to push air through a
higher-efficiency filter.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:52.880
- [Reporter] The air pumped into buildings

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.930
also needs to be cooled or heated,

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:56.900
which adds to energy bills.

00:04:56.900 --> 00:04:58.320
Even though not every building

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:00.210
can afford to make these adjustments,

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:02.580
experts say there are other solutions.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:04.820
For older buildings with outdated systems,

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:07.320
opening windows is a good, cheap option.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:09.680
But in the winter, this
isn't always possible,

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:12.130
and some buildings have
windows that are sealed shut.

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:14.780
- If you can't hit the targets,

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:16.760
consider supplemental air cleaning

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:18.230
through the use of a portable air cleaner

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:19.450
with a HEPA filter.

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:22.120
Now, if you size these
correctly for the room,

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:25.900
for restaurant, a school
classroom, a bedroom at home,

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:29.630
you can get four, five or
six air changes per hour

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:32.810
of clean air, and it
can be cost-effective.

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:34.930
- [Reporter] That's what the
DC public school system did,

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:37.990
in addition to upgrading
their ventilation systems.

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:40.400
The bottom line, we know
that the coronavirus

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:41.650
can spread through the air,

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:44.100
and we know how to reduce
the risk of transmission.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:45.780
The biggest hurdle will be the cost

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:47.815
of putting the science into action.

00:05:47.815 --> 00:05:50.898
(bouncy sting music)

